<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<!--
    Values, not appearances. Nothing in this file styles anything — it is the vocabulary the other three
    dictionaries and the pages spend.

    Why tokens rather than literals: every size here has to be reachable at runtime for text scaling to be
    possible at all (see the LargeUiScale work), and a literal in a page is a value no scale factor can find.
    That indirection is the whole mechanism, so it is worth the extra hop even where the value never changes.

    Consumers should use ThemeResource for anything that can change at runtime and StaticResource only for
    values that genuinely cannot.
-->
<ResourceDictionary
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

    <!--
        ================================================================================================
        Spacing — a 4 px scale

        XAML cannot do arithmetic, so each step exists twice: as an x:Double for Spacing/Width, and as a
        Thickness for Margin/Padding. Uniform Thicknesses only; an asymmetric one is a layout decision, not
        a token, and belongs at the site that needs it.

        THE RULE: vertical rhythm comes from a container's Spacing, never from a Margin on a text style.
        A margin baked into a style is invisible at the call site and cannot be overridden without
        redeclaring the style, which is exactly how this app ended up with two section-header styles
        differing only in their bottom margin — a difference nobody chose. Space between siblings belongs to
        whatever is arranging them.

        Deliberately absent: 6, 10 and 14, which the pages currently use ~30 times between them. They are
        off-grid, and blessing them as tokens would make the scale decorative. Migration snaps each to a
        real step as its page is touched, which is a visible change and therefore one to make deliberately
        rather than in a rename.
        ================================================================================================
    -->
    <x:Double x:Key="RipcordSpacingXXS">2</x:Double>
    <x:Double x:Key="RipcordSpacingXS">4</x:Double>
    <x:Double x:Key="RipcordSpacingS">8</x:Double>
    <x:Double x:Key="RipcordSpacingM">12</x:Double>
    <x:Double x:Key="RipcordSpacingL">16</x:Double>
    <x:Double x:Key="RipcordSpacingXL">24</x:Double>
    <x:Double x:Key="RipcordSpacingXXL">32</x:Double>

    <Thickness x:Key="RipcordInsetXXS">2</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="RipcordInsetXS">4</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="RipcordInsetS">8</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="RipcordInsetM">12</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="RipcordInsetL">16</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="RipcordInsetXL">24</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="RipcordInsetXXL">32</Thickness>

    <!--
        ================================================================================================
        Icon sizes

        Every FontSize literal in this app is on a FontIcon — there is not one on a TextBlock, because text
        goes through the type ramp in Ripcord.Text.xaml. So this is the complete set of size literals the
        app had, five tokens covering six values.

        Sized by RELATIONSHIP, which is what makes the set closed: an icon is either sitting in a line of
        text (and takes that text's size) or standing alone (and takes a size of its own).

          Caption  12 — inline with caption text: the decode pill, the play chevron on a card
          Body     14 — inline with body text: the About rows, a menu glyph
          Action   16 — beside a button's own label, which is body-sized; slightly larger on purpose, and
                        the one place a two-pixel difference is deliberate rather than drift
          Standing 18 — alone beside a paragraph: the About info mark, the touch controls
          Hero     24 — the empty state and the pair confirmation, where the glyph IS the content

        The single collapse: the pair check-mark was 22 and is now 24. Nothing else moves.
        ================================================================================================
    -->
    <x:Double x:Key="RipcordIconSizeCaption">12</x:Double>
    <x:Double x:Key="RipcordIconSizeBody">14</x:Double>
    <x:Double x:Key="RipcordIconSizeAction">16</x:Double>
    <x:Double x:Key="RipcordIconSizeStanding">18</x:Double>
    <x:Double x:Key="RipcordIconSizeHero">24</x:Double>

    <!--
        ================================================================================================
        Corner radii

        The vocabulary already in use across the app, named so the next surface picks from the set rather
        than inventing a seventh value.

        Two rules that go with them:

        1. NEVER override ControlCornerRadius or OverlayCornerRadius. Stock controls keep the system's
           radii — a button that is rounder here than everywhere else in Windows reads as a foreign app,
           and these tokens are for Ripcord's own surfaces only.
        2. A nested surface's radius must not exceed its parent's minus the padding between them, or the
           inner corner visibly crosses the outer one. This is why the set is graded rather than uniform.
        ================================================================================================
    -->
    <CornerRadius x:Key="RipcordTagCornerRadius">4</CornerRadius>
    <CornerRadius x:Key="RipcordCardCornerRadius">8</CornerRadius>
    <CornerRadius x:Key="RipcordPillCornerRadius">10</CornerRadius>
    <!--
        ================================================================================================
        Touch targets

        48 is the minimum a finger can hit reliably in app chrome, and it is the number every platform
        guideline converges on. It is a HIT AREA, not a visual size: a 32px glyph inside a 48px transparent
        button looks identical and is twice as easy to hit, which is why several of these are applied as
        MinWidth/MinHeight on a control whose background is transparent rather than as a redesign.

        40 is the compact case, used only where a container's own height caps it — the title bar is 48 tall,
        so a 48 button fills it edge to edge with no breathing room. Stated as its own token rather than
        written as a bare 40 somewhere, so the deviation is visible and countable.

        Over video the touch bar already proved 56×52, which is larger because there is no chrome around it to
        aim by and a mis-hit interrupts a game.
        ================================================================================================
    -->
    <x:Double x:Key="RipcordTouchTarget">48</x:Double>
    <x:Double x:Key="RipcordTouchTargetCompact">40</x:Double>

    <CornerRadius x:Key="RipcordPanelCornerRadius">12</CornerRadius>
    <CornerRadius x:Key="RipcordHeroCornerRadius">16</CornerRadius>

    <!--
        ================================================================================================
        Vendor accents

        The three dash colours of the Ripcord mark, one per console vendor — blue PlayStation, green Xbox,
        red Nintendo. See brand/README.md, which is the source of truth for both the values and the mapping.

        These are Ripcord's own values, chosen to read as "three platforms". They are NOT sampled from any
        console maker's brand guide, and must never be "corrected" toward an official one — the association
        is nominative, the same latitude NOTICE takes with the word "PS5", and it only holds while the
        values stay ours.

        Fixed in both themes on purpose: they are brand identity, not chrome, and both remain legible
        against Mica in light and dark. They are used for marks and low-opacity washes only, never as a text
        or an icon-on-fill colour, so they carry no contrast requirement of their own — which is also why
        colour is never the sole carrier of meaning anywhere it appears.

        In high contrast they must go away entirely: HC does not permit decorative colour outside the system
        palette. That is enforced in code rather than here, because a resource dictionary cannot read the
        setting — see AppEffects.
        ================================================================================================
    -->
    <Color x:Key="RipcordPlayStationAccentColor">#2D7DF6</Color>
    <Color x:Key="RipcordXboxAccentColor">#2FBF5B</Color>
    <Color x:Key="RipcordNintendoAccentColor">#F0433A</Color>

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="RipcordPlayStationAccentBrush" Color="{StaticResource RipcordPlayStationAccentColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="RipcordXboxAccentBrush" Color="{StaticResource RipcordXboxAccentColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="RipcordNintendoAccentBrush" Color="{StaticResource RipcordNintendoAccentColor}" />

</ResourceDictionary>
